  "zkArtifacts": {
    "_doc": "Canonical SHA-256 hashes for ZK circuit artifacts (wasm / zkey / vkey). Source of truth: this file, authored by Protocol Engineer from the post-ceremony artifacts that match the deployed Verifier contracts. Consumers (web) verify their bundled public/circuits/* copies against these \u2014 verifying a copy against itself is circular, so PE authors, web verifies. A pin is NEVER seeded from the artifact it pins: that manufactures agreement and is the exact mechanism by which web served the superseded April repayment VK for months while every check passed (S168; web #318 corrected it).",
    "_pendingSemantics": "TWO PENDINGS, TWO MEANINGS \u2014 do not let one flip the other. (1) HERE, proto zkArtifacts.<Circuit>.pending answers 'are the canonical hashes recorded?'. It flips false the moment PE records them, and says nothing about what any consumer serves. (2) WEB, public/deployments/stack1-arbitrum-one-v0.json zkArtifacts.<circuit>.pending answers 'is the served artifact set complete enough to generate a proof?' \u2014 web's repayment.pending stays TRUE until the zkey ships web-side, which web #318 left deliberately. As of S168 proto Repayment is pending=false and web repayment is pending=true, and BOTH are correct simultaneously. A future session that reads one and updates the other has produced a defect, not a sync. Invariant (aa) enforces hash identity across the ledgers; it deliberately does NOT couple the two pending flags.",
    "_ledgerIdentity": "These entries mirror circuits/canonical-hashes.json and are checked against it by Pattern A invariant (aa) \u2014 same circuit, same artifact role, same hash, or CI fails. Cross-repo identity against web's zkArtifacts is checked by scripts/check-manifest-exhaustiveness.py. Both exist because this block is a MIRROR of registry state (class-3), and an unchecked mirror is how ledgers silently diverge.",
